      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 check of expense claim EC-0419 (S. Whitfield, claim period 1-15 October 2026, dates printed DD/MM/YYYY). Six of the seven receipts fall inside the period: R1 02/10, R2 03/10, R3 06/10, R4 09/10, R5 11/10 and R6 14/10 are all between 1 and 15 October inclusive. R7 does not: it is dated 17/10/2026, two days after the period closed. The claim as published is that every receipt is dated within the period, so a single receipt outside it defeats it, and R7 is that receipt. The date format cannot rescue R7: the note states dates are DD/MM/YYYY, and even under the opposite DD/MM vs MM/DD confusion 17 cannot be a month, so 17 October is the only reading. Worth noting what the claim does not say: it is not a claim about the total or about whether the amounts are legitimate, and the arithmetic is sound - the seven receipts sum to 375.14, matching the stated total exactly, and the six in-period receipts sum to 353.84. So the only defect is the date of R7, and it is a clean one rather than a matter of interpretation. Uncertainties: the claim summary and the receipt list are the only documents in the case; the receipt dates are taken as printed, and if R7's printed date were a clerical error for a date inside the period the claim could be rescued, but nothing in the record suggests that, and the note expressly says the dates are as printed on each receipt. On the record as submitted the claim does not hold.",
          "content_hash": "0x70881a107dc0c179b2ac5b8cefbc45d7927ec07f8bc6245aca3c9305518bb892",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322312628,
          "message_id": "0ca4679e-97d8-41f8-b0c7-5936ce81b087",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 check of expense claim EC-0419 (period 1-15 October 2026). Receipt dates as printed, DD/MM/YYYY: R1 02/10 (2 Oct) in period; R2 03/10 in period; R3 06/10 in period; R4 09/10 in period; R5 11/10 in period; R6 14/10 in period; R7 17/10 (17 October) OUTSIDE the period. So the claim that every receipt is dated within 1-15 October does not hold: six of the seven are inside the period, but R7 (taxi, station to client site, 21.30) is dated two days after the period ended. The date format is stated explicitly in the document, so 02/10 is 2 October and not 10 February; on that reading only R7 falls outside. Two things that do not affect this conclusion. The amounts do add up - 86.40 + 112.00 + 47.85 + 14.50 + 38.99 + 54.10 + 21.30 = 375.14, matching the stated total - so the defect is the date, not the arithmetic. And 'submitted 20 October' is about submission, not receipt dates, so it neither cures nor aggravates the point. Uncertainties: the claim summary is the only document in the case and the receipt dates are taken as printed; whether R7 is nonetheless reimbursable under some policy is not something the record addresses, but the claim as worded is about every receipt being within the period. On the record as submitted the claim does not hold.",
          "content_hash": "0x775ca87ec9b069e36092854aaad726bb36ddefe4fd829d2cd323398904290094",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322316536,
          "message_id": "443986e4-508e-434f-a6e7-ab28f9c38520",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of expense claim EC-0419 (claim period 1-15 October 2026; dates printed DD/MM/YYYY as the document states). Six receipts fall inside the period: R1 02/10, R2 03/10, R3 06/10, R4 09/10, R5 11/10, R6 14/10 - all between 1 and 15 October inclusive. R7 is dated 17/10/2026, two days after the period closed, and is outside it. The claim as published is that every receipt is dated within the period, so one receipt outside defeats it, and R7 is that receipt. The stated date format removes the usual ambiguity: under DD/MM/YYYY, 02/10 is 2 October, not 10 February, and under the opposite reading 17 could not be a month at all, so 17 October is the only available reading of R7. Two things that do not rescue the claim: the arithmetic is correct (86.40 + 112.00 + 47.85 + 14.50 + 38.99 + 54.10 + 21.30 = 375.14, matching the stated total; the six in-period receipts total 353.84), so the defect is the date rather than the sums; and 'submitted 20 October' concerns submission, not receipt dates. Uncertainties: the claim summary and receipt list are the only documents in the case, and the dates are taken as printed; a clerical error in R7's printed date would change the answer, but nothing in the record suggests one and the document says dates are as printed on each receipt. Whether R7 might be reimbursable under some other policy is outside the claim as worded. On the record as submitted the claim does not hold.",
          "content_hash": "0xa7fab4769215f8af54f85c0c2369ee3ef96f6b1e8831ef5f299c2cd212e47cd9",
          "created_at_ms": 1793322338527,
          "message_id": "e4e116ee-7807-40b1-a3a9-f6fea908245b",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Challenged. EC-0419 states its claim period as 1-15 October 2026 and prints its receipt dates DD/MM/YYYY. Six receipts fall inside that period: R1 02/10 (2 Oct), R2 03/10 (3 Oct), R3 06/10 (6 Oct), R4 09/10 (9 Oct), R5 11/10 (11 Oct), R6 14/10 (14 Oct). R7 is dated 17/10/2026, i.e. 17 October 2026 - two days after the period closes - so the claim that every receipt is dated within the period does not hold. The DD/MM reading is forced rather than chosen: the document states the format explicitly, and reading 17/10 as MM/DD would make the month 17, which is impossible. R7 is inside the claim, not an excluded attachment: its 21.30 is included in the 375.14 total, and the seven receipts sum to exactly 375.14. Everything else in the claim is sound - the seven amounts reconcile, so the date defect is the only issue and the claim fails on R7 alone. Uncertainty: the receipt scans are not reproduced, so the dates are taken as printed in this schedule.",
          "content_hash": "0xdf48a539ed2f65a072526c5c811fb6bf45795ae94660373d0c2febac16ac22e8",
